Introduction
The previous part
moved every risk control in the series into a framework of three classes, one for each scope of risk, and closed by suggesting that a trailing stop could become another class alongside them, with its own configuration structure and an additional call in the tick handler. This part builds that class and connects it to the Series EA. The broker's freeze level was set aside in the preceding parts because it governs changes to an order or position that already exists, and nothing in the EA moved a stop. A trailing stop changes the stop of an open position on every move, so the freeze level is checked here as well, against the stop and target the position already carries.
By the end of this part, you will have:
TrailingStop.mqh in the Risk Management folder, holding one engine and two trailing methods.
An ATR trail and an R-multiple ladder, with break-even as a floor under either.
The Series EA trailing its own positions on every tick while its signal is still read once per bar.
Problem: A Stop Set at Entry and Never Revisited
Every control built so far runs before a trade exists. Sizing, the margin checks, the basket limits, and account limits all decide whether a trade may open and how large it may be. After the order is sent, the EA never looks at the stop again. The basket tier can close a losing group of positions, but nothing moves an individual stop. A position that has traveled a long way in its favor, therefore, still carries the stop it was opened with. If the price turns and comes back, and nothing else closes the position first, the trade gives up all of its open profit and then loses the full amount it risked at entry. The take-profit sits at a fixed multiple of the stop distance, and until price reaches it, nothing protects what the trade has gained.
A trailing stop moves the stop behind the price as the trade progresses. Doing that safely is a different problem from the ones solved so far. A pre-trade check that gets something wrong costs a trade, or lets one through at the wrong size. A stop modification that gets something wrong can widen the risk on a live position, be refused by the trade server, or keep resending a refused request on every tick.
Two Questions in Every Trailing Stop
On every evaluation, a trailing stop answers two separate questions. The first is where the stop should move. This is the part that differs between methods. An ATR trail answers it with a distance scaled to recent volatility. An R-multiple ladder answers it with a fixed set of profit milestones. Either calculation is only a few lines of arithmetic. The second is whether the stop may move to that level right now. The engine checks the same four rules for every method before it sends anything:
Rule
Why it applies
The stop only moves in the protective direction.
A trailing stop that loosens increases the risk on the position.
The new level respects the broker's stop level.
The server rejects a stop placed too close to the current price.
The price is outside the freeze distance of the current stop and target.
The server refuses any change to a stop or target that price is about to reach.
The move is at least a minimum step.
Each modification is a request to the trade server, and moving a stop by a fraction of a point costs a request while protecting almost nothing.
The framework keeps these two questions separate. Each method answers the first one and returns a price, or zero when it has no proposal. The engine answers the second one for every method, and it is the only code that sends a modification. A method is given no trade object and is never asked to send anything, so every level it proposes goes through the same checks. The checks cover the stop's direction, the stop level, the freeze distance to the current stop and target, and the minimum move size. The checks do not cover the symbol's trade mode, terminal/account trading permissions, tick-size alignment, or the freeze distance to the proposed level. Therefore, a request can still be refused even if it passes all four checks. The engine therefore also reads the server's answer, as the section on sending the modification shows.
Splitting trailing into interchangeable methods that only calculate a level is a well-established design. The engine in this part also checks the proposed stop against the stop level, and the position's current stop and target against the freeze level, before sending, and reads R from the trade history for the features that need it.
Scope of This Article
One header file is added to the Risk Management folder, and the Series EA includes it. The signal, the sizing, the plan-validate-execute pipeline, and the three risk tiers are unchanged. The header adds:
A configuration structure and a context structure describing one position.
A base class for trailing methods, with an ATR method and an R-multiple ladder.
Break-even as a floor under the active method or on its own.
One engine that owns the four checks, reads R from history, and sends every modification.
The EA adds two input groups, a configuration block in
OnInit()
, one call at the top of
OnTick()
, and an
OnTradeTransaction()
handler. The first section below covers the header on its own. The second covers the EA.
Section 1: Building TrailingStop.mqh
The Configuration
The trailing class follows the same pattern as the trade and basket tiers: one configuration structure, filled once by the EA, with a default value for every field.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Trailing configuration, filled once by the EA                    |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
STrailConfig
  {
long
magic;
   ENUM_TRAIL_METHOD method;
double
activateAtR;
// 0 = act as soon as a stop can improve
//--- Break-even
bool
useBreakEven;
double
breakEvenAtR;
// Profit in R that moves the stop to entry
int
breakEvenOffsetPoints;
// Fixed points beyond entry, not the actual costs
//--- ATR method
double
atrMultiplier;
//--- R-ladder method
double
ladderStepR;
// Rung spacing, in R
double
ladderLagR;
// How far behind the reached rung to lock, in R
//--- Guards and logging
int
minStepPoints;
// Ignore improvements smaller than this
bool
verbose;
// Log every modification
void
SetDefaults(
void
);
  };
method
selects which built-in method the engine creates.
activateAtR
holds everything back until the position is that many R in profit, and zero lets the engine act as soon as a stop can improve.
minStepPoints
is the smallest move the engine will send. The other fields are covered with the code that reads them.
