The defaults switch both the method and break-even off, so an engine that is included and never configured moves nothing.
What a Method Is Given
For each position it evaluates, the engine fills one context structure and passes it to the method as read-only input.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Everything a method may read about one position                  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
STrailContext
  {
string
symbol;
ulong
ticket;
ENUM_POSITION_TYPE
positionType;
double
entryPrice;
double
currentSL;
double
currentTP;
double
bid;
double
ask;
double
closePrice;
// Bid for a buy, Ask for a sell
double
profitDistance;
// How far closePrice is beyond entry
double
originalRisk;
// R in price units, 0 when unknown
double
atrValue;
double
point;
int
digits;
  };
closePrice
is Bid for a buy and Ask for a sell, because that is the price the position's stop is tested against.
profitDistance
is how far that price has moved beyond entry, and it is negative while the position is losing.
originalRisk
is R, the distance between entry and the stop the position was opened with, and it is zero when the engine does not know it.
symbol
and
ticket
identify the position, and the
point
and
digits
carry the symbol's precision. The convention is that a method reads only from this structure. If a later method needs information that is not here, the change is a new field, and the engine stays the only code that reads positions and market prices.
