//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Trail at a volatility-scaled distance behind the closing side    |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CTrailATR::ComputeStopLevel(
const
STrailContext &ctx)
  {
if
(ctx.atrValue <=
0
|| m_multiplier <=
0
)
return
0.0
;
double
distance = ctx.atrValue * m_multiplier;
if
(ctx.positionType ==
POSITION_TYPE_BUY
)
return
ctx.closePrice - distance;
return
ctx.closePrice + distance;
  }
The ATR method places the stop a multiple of ATR behind the closing price. The value it uses is whatever the EA passes to
Manage()
. The Series EA refreshes it once per bar, as
Section 2
shows, so the distance changes at most once per bar while the closing price it is measured from moves on every tick. It proposes a new level on every evaluation, and the minimum-step check decides which of those proposals are large enough to send. When the next bar brings a larger ATR, the proposal falls further behind price and the stop stays where it is, since the engine never moves a stop backwards. When ATR falls, the proposal moves closer, and the stop can advance faster than the price does.
The initial stop and the trail use different multipliers. With the EA's defaults, the initial stop is 1.5 ATR from entry, and the trail follows 2.0 ATR behind price. If ATR has not changed since entry, the first move comes only after price has gone about half an ATR in the trade's favor, plus the minimum step. Setting the trailing multiplier at or below the initial stop multiplier makes the trail start sooner.
R and R-Multiple Ladder
R is the distance between a position's entry price and the stop-loss it was opened with. The engine measures it once, from the order that opened the position, so it assumes the entry price does not change, which holds unless a netting position is added to. A trade that is one R in profit has moved as far in its favor as its stop was placed against it. The Series EA sets its take-profit at
TPRatio
times the stop distance, which makes the default target 2R. The ladder moves the stop in fixed milestones measured in R.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| R-multiple ladder trailing                                       |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
class
CTrailRRLadder :
public
CTrailMethod
  {
private
:
double
m_stepR;
double
m_lagR;
public
:
                     CTrailRRLadder(
const
double
stepR,
const
double
lagR)
     {
      m_stepR = stepR;
      m_lagR  = lagR;
     }
virtual
double
ComputeStopLevel(
const
STrailContext &ctx);
virtual
bool
NeedsOriginalRisk(
void
)
const
{
return
true
; }
virtual
string
