The table illustrates the calculation using those settings. It is not a result from a test run.
If
TPRatio
is set at its default of 2.0, the position closes at 2R, the same level at which the ladder would make its first move, so with the default settings, the ladder adds nothing beyond break-even. A smaller ladder step, such as 0.5R, would create additional rungs between whole R multiples. For the ladder to lock profit, the take-profit has to sit beyond the rungs it should reach. A TPRatio of 4.0, as an example, leaves room for rungs at 2R and 3R.
The minimum step applies to the ladder as well. When one rung is worth fewer points than
minStepPoints
, the move to the next rung is too small to send, and the stop stays where it is until the move to a later rung clears the minimum, skipping the rungs in between. With a 1R step and a stop several hundred points wide, a rung is far larger than the default minimum of 50 points, so this arises only with a small step or a very tight stop.
The figure uses an illustrative price path, with the ATR trail running alone and break-even at 1R combined with the ladder. The ATR stop follows price in small steps and gives part of each advance back to volatility. The ladder ignores everything between rungs and moves in whole R.
