The stop recorded on the opening order cannot change, so R is cached, and the history is read once per position. Entries for closed positions are removed at the end of each full pass, so the cache holds at most one entry for every position this EA has open. A call made during a refusal pause returns before it reads any position, so it neither adds nor removes anything. A pass cut short by a refusal can add entries but removes none, because it has not seen every open position.
If the history holds no orders for the position yet, which can happen just after a position opens, or its stop cannot be read, nothing is cached, and the lookup runs again on the next evaluation. If the order was sent without a stop, R is zero, a log line reports it, and every R-based feature leaves that position alone. A position whose opening order is missing from a history that holds its other orders is treated the same way, with its own log line. Both results are cached, so the R-based features stay off for that position until it closes, even if the missing order reaches the history later.
The two log lines tell the cases apart, and caching avoids a history query on every tick for as long as the position stays open. The Series EA always sends its stop with the order, so a missing stop only affects a position with the EA's magic number that was opened some other way. After a terminal restart the cache is empty, and the first evaluation reads the same value from the history again, as long as the terminal still holds that position's order history.
Break-Even as a Floor
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Break-even level for this position, or 0 if not reached yet      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CTrailingStop::BreakEvenLevel(
const
STrailContext &ctx)
const
{
if
(!m_cfg.useBreakEven || ctx.originalRisk <=
0
)
return
0.0
;
if
(ctx.profitDistance < m_cfg.breakEvenAtR * ctx.originalRisk)
return
0.0
;
double
offset = m_cfg.breakEvenOffsetPoints * ctx.point;
if
(ctx.positionType ==
POSITION_TYPE_BUY
)
return
ctx.entryPrice + offset;
return
ctx.entryPrice - offset;
  }
Once profit reaches
breakEvenAtR
times R, break-even proposes a stop at entry plus an offset. In this article, break-even means that level, entry plus a fixed number of points, and not a guarantee of closing without a loss. A stop exactly at entry closes at the entry price, so what a position still loses there is commission, swap, and any slippage. The offset leaves room for those, but it does not read them, so on an account with high commission, or on a position that has paid swap for several days, the default may not cover them.
Furthermore, the engine combines break-even with the active method by taking the better of the two levels: the higher one for a buy and the lower one for a sell. Break-even, therefore, acts as a floor. With the EA's defaults, an unchanged ATR, and a buy, the ATR trail starts lifting the stop before 1R, break-even raises it to entry plus the offset at 1R, and the ATR level takes over again once it exceeds that level by at least the minimum step, which occurs a little past 1.3R. Break-even also works with the method set to off.
The Four Checks
Order
Check
Rejects when
1
Improvement
The level does not move the stop in the protective direction.
2
Minimum step
The move is smaller than minStepPoints.
3
Stops level
The level is closer to the closing price than the broker allows.
4
Freeze level
The price is within the freeze distance of the current stop or target.
The level is rounded to the symbol's precision before any of them run, so each check measures the price the server will receive rather than one that rounding can still move. Prices on the symbol's grid differ by whole points, so floating-point error can only matter for a level exactly on a boundary. Such a level is either skipped until the price moves on, or sent at a distance the documented rules accept.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| A stop may only move in the protective direction                 |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CTrailingStop::IsImprovement(
const
STrailContext &ctx,
const
double
proposed)
const
{
if
(ctx.currentSL <=
0
)
return
true
;
if
(ctx.positionType ==
POSITION_TYPE_BUY
)
return
(proposed > ctx.currentSL);
return
(proposed < ctx.currentSL);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Is the improvement large enough to send?                         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CTrailingStop::ClearsMinimumStep(
const
STrailContext &ctx,
const
double
proposed)
const
{
if
(ctx.currentSL <=
0
)
return
true
;
if
(m_cfg.minStepPoints <=
0
)
return
true
;
double
improvement =
MathAbs
(proposed - ctx.currentSL);
return
(improvement >= m_cfg.minStepPoints * ctx.point);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| The server rejects a stop too close to, or beyond, the price     |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CTrailingStop::RespectsStopsLevel(
const
STrailContext &ctx,
const
double
proposed)
const
{
//--- Even with no broker minimum, the stop must sit at least a point on the protective side
double
minDistance =
MathMax
(StopsLevelPrice(), ctx.point);
//--- A buy's stop is tested against Bid, a sell's against Ask
double
distance = (ctx.positionType ==
POSITION_TYPE_BUY
)
                     ? ctx.bid - proposed
                     : proposed - ctx.ask;
return
(distance >= minDistance);
  }
The improvement check accepts any proposed level when the position has no stop. Otherwise, it requires a strict improvement, so a method that calculates a bad level can fail to help but can never move an existing stop against the position. A position without a stop skips the improvement and minimum-step checks and takes the first level that clears the stop level and freeze checks. Those set a minimum distance from price and no maximum, so no check judges whether that first stop is a sensible risk for the position. The minimum step is in points, and zero switches it off, following the convention used throughout the framework.
The stop level is measured from Bid for a buy and from Ask for a sell. At entry, the Series EA required the stop distance to cover the stop level plus the spread because a buy enters at Ask while its stop is tested at Bid. Here the distance is measured from the closing price directly, so the spread is already included. A reported stop level of zero means the broker sets no minimum distance, but a stop at or beyond the current price is still invalid, so the check never accepts a distance smaller than one point.
The Freeze Level
SYMBOL_TRADE_FREEZE_LEVEL is the distance, in points, within which the server treats a position's stop or target as about to trigger and refuses to change either of them. For a buy position, Bid must be at least that distance from both the stop and the target before a modification is accepted. For a sell, the same applies to Ask.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Modification is refused while price is inside the freeze level   |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CTrailingStop::IsFrozen(
const
STrailContext &ctx)
const
{
double
freezeLevel = FreezeLevelPrice();
if
(freezeLevel <=
0
)
return
false
;
if
(ctx.currentSL >
0
&&
MathAbs
(ctx.closePrice - ctx.currentSL) < freezeLevel)
return
true
;
if
(ctx.currentTP >
0
&&
MathAbs
(ctx.closePrice - ctx.currentTP) < freezeLevel)
return
true
;
return
false
;
  }
The check measures the stop and the target the position already carries, which is what the server refuses to change. It does not measure the level being proposed. When it triggers, the engine sends nothing and, with verbose on, logs that the position is inside the freeze level, once per bar for each position.
VerboseThisBar()
keeps that count: it remembers the bar and the positions already logged on it, and starts a new list when the bar changes. The next tick is evaluated normally, so the stop moves as soon as the price leaves the freeze zone. When a symbol reports a freeze level of zero, the check does nothing. The value can be found in the symbol specification in MetaTrader. These conditions are also described in the MQL5 article
"The checks a trading robot must pass before publication in the market"
by
MetaQuotes
, for those looking to publish their work on the MQL5 Marketplace.
Sending the Modification
