true
;
  }
The level is rounded to the symbol's digits, and a request identical to the current stop is not sent. The take-profit is passed back unchanged, because
PositionModify()
sets both levels at once and a zero would remove the target. A true return from
PositionModify()
means the request passed
CTrade's
own checks and was sent, not that the server applied it. The return code carries that answer, so both are read, and anything other than TRADE_RETCODE_DONE or TRADE_RETCODE_PLACED is treated as a refusal. Without the second test, a line reporting a moved stop could be printed for a request the server declined. Some refusals clear on the next tick, such as one caused by price sitting too close to the level. Others do not change until something outside the EA does, such as trading being switched off in the terminal.
The engine treats both the same way. It records the current bar and leaves the loop, so no further position is modified in that pass, and every later call returns at its first check until a new bar opens. Without that, a request that keeps being refused would be sent again on every tick, identical or with a level that has moved slightly. The cost falls on the temporary case, where the stop stays at its last accepted level until the next bar, which on a higher timeframe can be a while. With verbose on, each accepted modification
prints one line
.
The Evaluation Loop
The evaluation loop returns at once when there is neither a method nor break-even or while a refusal pause is in effect. It reads the tick once and decides whether R is needed at all, so an ATR trail with both break-even and the activation threshold disabled never accesses the history. For each of the EA's positions on the symbol, it fills the context, applies the activation threshold, combines the proposal with the break-even floor, and runs the four checks. When the activation threshold is set and R is unknown, the position is left alone.
